<div class="wrap">
<header class="top">
  <div class="eyebrow">JusPrin · jusprin-newui · 2026-10-28</div>
  <h1>JusPrin Two-Flow Walkthrough</h1>
  <p class="lede">Six scenarios run in the built app with Claude Opus 5.5, one screenshot per step, judged from the seat of someone who owns a Bambu A1 mini, has used a slicer before, and has never seen JusPrin's source or design documents.</p>
  <div class="meta">
    <span><b>Build</b> a684aee37d (RelWithDebInfo, local Mac)</span>
    <span><b>Model</b> Claude Opus 5.5, Thinking: Default</span>
    <span><b>Printer</b> in-process fake A1 mini, selected and idle</span>
    <span><b>Files</b> two MakerWorld 3MFs, one Printables-style STL</span>
    <span><b>Frames</b> 34</span>
  </div>
  <nav class="toc">
    <a href="#verdict">Verdict</a><a href="#findings">Findings</a><a href="#persona">The person</a><a href="#launch">Launch</a>
    <a href="#s1a">1A cable holder</a><a href="#s2a">2A issue chip</a><a href="#s1b">1B bare STL</a><a href="#s2c">2C filament by hand</a><a href="#s1c">1C wrong-printer file</a><a href="#s2b">2B too big</a>
    <a href="#good">What worked</a><a href="#setup">Test setup</a>
  </nav>
</header>

<h2 id="verdict">Verdict on the two entry points</h2>
<div class="verdict">
  <div>
    <h4>Flow 1, open a model and print it <span class="pill good">natural</span></h4>
    <p>Home's drop zone and <span class="ui">Open…</span> button are where anyone would look, and once the file opens the chat speaks first, so nobody has to discover a chat. The first message is the strongest thing in the product: it names the model, its size, whether it fits, what the file changed, and asks one question with buttons. Two things break the entry before it starts: OrcaSlicer's own dialogs at launch when a connected printer disagrees with the resumed project, and a wall of settings "edits" attributed to the person when a second project is opened.</p>
  </div>
  <div>
    <h4>Flow 2, fix a problem with the plan <span class="pill med">findable, not obvious</span></h4>
    <p>There are three ways in, and they disagree. The agent's post-slice report is the best one and arrives on its own. The <span class="ui">N print issues</span> chip in the canvas corner is small, far from the chat, keeps counting warnings the agent has already dismissed, and offers <span class="ui">Ask AI</span> and <span class="ui">Resolve with AI</span> without saying how they differ. For a setup the person changed by hand there is no entry at all: the card says <span class="ui">Plan needs another look</span> and nothing else happens until they type.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 id="findings">Findings, most serious first</h2>
<p>Severity is from the person's seat: how likely they are to hit it on a normal day, and how badly it misleads them.</p>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill crit">critical</span><h4>Two OrcaSlicer dialogs block Home at launch</h4><span class="where">Launch · frames 0 and 1</span></div>
<p>With a Bambu printer connected and a project behind Home whose profile is for another printer, the app opens with a "Tips" dialog about syncing "the project preset", and answering Yes opens "Transfer or discard changes" for a preset of a printer the person does not own, listing settings they never touched. Both are in OrcaSlicer's wording and layout. The person has not opened anything yet, does not know a project is open behind Home, and has to guess between Save (which writes a modified printer preset) and Discard. This is the first impression of the app for every Bambu owner whose last project came from MakerWorld.</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill crit">critical</span><h4>Opening a bare STL shows nine "edits" attributed to the person</h4><span class="where">1B · frame 1B-02</span></div>
<p>The new project's chat opens with rows like <span class="ui">Sparse infill density 20% → 15% · you</span>, <span class="ui">Inherits profile 0.20mm Standard @BBL A1M → Empty</span> and <span class="ui">print_settings_id HSW_Kabelhalter → Empty</span>. They are the previous project's embedded settings being reset, but the person sees raw setting keys, a preset name from a file they closed, and the word "you" on changes they never made. They have no way to tell whether this matters.</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill high">high</span><h4>Pressing Send gives no acknowledgement</h4><span class="where">1A · frame 1A-12</span></div>
<p>After Send and its warning, the dialog closes and nothing else changes: no line in the chat, no change in the header, the issue chip still shows. The log confirms the job reached the printer. The person has no way to know. In real use the printer would start moving a minute later, but the app should say "Sent to A1 mini" the moment it knows.</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill high">high</span><h4>Print opens a dialog in another product's voice, with an error, then a warning</h4><span class="where">1A · frames 1A-08 to 1A-11</span></div>
<p>The agent says "ready to print"; Print then shows OrcaSlicer's send dialog with a red "Not all filaments used in slicing are mapped to the printer" error, a "?" slot to pick, and after Send another "Warning" dialog with Confirm. Nothing in the chat prepared the person for mapping, and the chat is unreachable while the dialog is open. Part of the mapping trouble is the fake printer's unknown spool, but an unread AMS slot is a common real state too.</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill high">high</span><h4>The issue chip contradicts the agent</h4><span class="where">1A, 1B, 2B · frames 1A-05, 1B-05, 2B-03</span></div>
<p>The agent writes "nothing to fix" and the canvas shows <span class="ui">1 print issue</span> with an orange dot. In 1B the agent says "two warnings, neither needs action" and the chip says 3. The Check print window says "Checked, nothing found … OrcaSlicer warnings" while the chip stays lit under it. A number badge means unresolved problems to anyone who has used software; here it means "OrcaSlicer emitted text".</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill high">high</span><h4>A file for another printer switches the printer silently</h4><span class="where">1C · frame 1C-01</span></div>
<p>Opening a MakerWorld 3MF made for a P1S changes the header chip to <span class="ui">Bambu Lab P1S · 0.4</span> and draws the 256 mm P1S bed, although the person owns only an A1 mini and the printer list has no P1S. Only the chat explains it, fifteen seconds later. Anyone who glances at the header and slices gets G-code for a printer they do not have. At launch the same situation produced OrcaSlicer's sync dialog instead; the two behaviours do not match.</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill med">medium</span><h4>After a change by hand, the agent stays silent until spoken to</h4><span class="where">2C · frames 2C-02, 2C-03</span></div>
<p>Switching the filament to ABS from the chip marks the card <span class="ui">⚠ Plan needs another look · Generic ABS @System</span>, but nothing invites the person to act, and a manual Slice completes with ABS and no comment. Once asked, the answer is excellent (warping, cracking, fumes on an open printer, three options). The warning on the card also disappears the moment the agent starts working, before the person has decided anything.</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill med">medium</span><h4>Ask AI and Resolve with AI, with no difference stated</h4><span class="where">2A · frame 2A-01</span></div>
<p>The chip's popup shows OrcaSlicer's text ("open the front door and/or remove the upper glass", advice for an enclosed printer) with two AI buttons and an "Ignore this warning" link. The person cannot tell what Resolve would do that Ask would not. Ask AI then posts a canned message in the person's name, "Explain this issue and my options", which is fine but unannounced.</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill med">medium</span><h4>"1 failed" in the activity row, never explained</h4><span class="where">2A · frame 2A-03</span></div>
<p>The row under the answer reads <span class="ui">Worked for 4s · 1 action · 1 failed</span>. The answer itself is fine, but "failed" with no sentence about what failed makes a careful person distrust the answer. The app log carried no line for it either, so the failure is invisible to developers too.</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill med">medium</span><h4>The chat does not scroll to a new answer</h4><span class="where">2C · frame 2C-04</span></div>
<p>After sending "Is it still fine to print like this?", the chat stayed on the old plan; the question and the answer were below the fold and took a long scroll to reach. Seen once, on one message; worth reproducing.</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill med">medium</span><h4>The agent claims changes the app made</h4><span class="where">1A, 2A · frames 1A-03, 2A-03</span></div>
<p>"When I opened the file, I switched the filament setting from PLA Matte to PLA Basic" (the file's settings did that) and "I show this warning for every PLA print" (OrcaSlicer does). Small, but it teaches the person a wrong model of who does what.</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill med">medium</span><h4>Names that do not match: Untitled, Tape_4.STL, New chat</h4><span class="where">all · frames 1A-02, 1A-07, 1A-04</span></div>
<p>The project is "universal_cable_holder_v2" in the title bar, the plate says <span class="ui">Untitled</span> in orange on the canvas, Check print calls the object <span class="ui">Tape_4.STL</span> (the file name inside the 3MF), and the chat is "New chat" until it renames itself "Confirmation to Proceed With Slicing". Three names for one thing, and a chat title that changes under the person.</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill med">medium</span><h4>"9 settings changed" with nothing to compare against</h4><span class="where">1A, 1B · setup card</span></div>
<p>The card says 9, then 5, then 2 settings changed. Changed from what, by whom? The person who just opened a downloaded file reads it as "something is already wrong".</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill low">low</span><h4>Check print sits under the Print button's chevron</h4><span class="where">1A · frame 1A-06</span></div>
<p>A slicer user expects a Preview tab. Here Check Print, Print all plates (disabled, "1 sliced") and Export sliced file live in a menu attached to Print. Findable in one click, but the disabled row gives no reason.</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill low">low</span><h4>The agent sometimes asks before slicing and sometimes does not</h4><span class="where">1A vs 1B</span></div>
<p>With the cable holder it asked "Should I slice it with these settings?"; with the hook it sliced on its own after the purpose was given. Either is defensible; alternating is what confuses.</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill low">low</span><h4>Composer shows model and "Thinking: Default" pickers</h4><span class="where">all · composer</span></div>
<p>"Claude Opus 5.5" and "Thinking: Default" sit beside every message. A person with printer knowledge and no AI knowledge wonders whether they are supposed to change these.</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill low">low</span><h4>After the two-plate fix the camera lands on plate 2</h4><span class="where">2B · frame 2B-03</span></div>
<p>The final view shows plate 2 with one headband piece; the arrow on plate 1 is cut off at the left edge. The card says "13 g · Plate 1" while the agent says plate 1 is 12.9 g and plate 2 is 1.5 g.</p></div>

<div class="finding"><div class="t"><span class="pill low">low</span><h4>Filament menu stays open after choosing</h4><span class="where">2C · frame 2C-02</span></div>
<p>Picking Generic ABS updates the chip but leaves the menu open until the person clicks elsewhere.</p></div>

<h2 id="persona">The person in the seat</h2>
<p>Every "what they know" line below is written for one person: they own an A1 mini with the AMS lite, have printed a few things from Bambu Studio, download models from MakerWorld and Printables, and know words like infill, supports, PLA, brim. They have never read a JusPrin document. They do not know what a preset, a profile, a process, or "@System" is, and they do not know that OrcaSlicer is underneath.</p>

<h2 id="launch">Launch, before any scenario</h2>
<p>The app was started with a printer connected and the last project (a MakerWorld headband for a P1S) resumed behind Home. That is what a returning user gets.</p>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/00-launch-home-orca-sync-tips-dialog.jpg" alt="Home page behind a Tips dialog asking whether to sync the printer information and switch the preset"><figcaption><span class="n">Frame 0</span> Home, with OrcaSlicer's "Tips" dialog in the middle.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They launched the app. They own an A1 mini and it is on.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>Home behind a dialog: "Connected printer is Bambu Lab A1 mini. It must match the project preset for printing. Do you want to sync the printer information and automatically switch the preset?" Yes, No, "Don't show again".</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>Which project? They have not opened one. The title bar says find_my_hollow, which Home does not show as open.</li><li>"Project preset" and "sync the printer information" are not their words.</li><li>No idea what Yes changes. Most people click Yes because it sounds helpful.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/01-launch-orca-transfer-or-discard-dialog.jpg" alt="Transfer or discard changes dialog listing G-code thumbnails and Maximum Junction for a P1S preset"><figcaption><span class="n">Frame 1</span> After Yes: "Transfer or discard changes" for a Bambu Lab P1S preset.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They just said Yes to a question about their A1 mini. They have changed nothing.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>"You have changed some settings of preset 'Bambu Lab P1S 0.4 nozzle'", a table with "G-code thumbnails" and "Maximum Junction 0.01 / 0.01", a Help link, Discard and Save.</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>They do not own a P1S.</li><li>They did not change settings, and the two listed look identical in both columns.</li><li>Save sounds safe and is the harmful choice: it writes a modified printer preset.</li><li>The dialog looks like a different product (it is OrcaSlicer's).</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/02-home-after-dialogs.jpg" alt="JusPrin Home page with a project grid, a drop zone, Open and New buttons, and a printers column"><figcaption><span class="n">Frame 2</span> Home once the dialogs are gone.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They want to print a file they downloaded.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>A drop zone listing file types, a project grid with thumbnails, <span class="ui">+ New</span> and <span class="ui">Open…</span>, and a printers column where their fake A1 mini says "Idle" and six others say "Not connected".</p></div>
    <div class="ok"><div class="k">Judgement</div><ul><li>Clear. Open… and the drop zone are exactly where they would look.</li><li>Every tile says "Draft" with no other state ever shown, so the word carries no information.</li><li>The title bar still names a project while Home is showing; that project is invisible here.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 id="s1a">Scenario 1A · a MakerWorld file made for their printer</h2>
<p>File: "universal cable holder V2" from MakerWorld, saved by its designer with a Bambu A1 mini profile, PLA Basic, textured plate, 20% infill. The best case: the file matches the printer.</p>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1A-01-open-file-chooser.jpg" alt="macOS file chooser opened from Home's Open button"><figcaption><span class="n">1A-01</span> Open… shows the standard macOS chooser.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>The file is in Downloads.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>The normal Mac file dialog with "Choose one or more files (3MF/STEP/STL/SVG/OBJ/AMF/USD*/ABC/PLY)".</p></div>
    <div class="ok"><div class="k">Judgement</div><p>Nothing to learn. Fine.</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1A-02-prepare-agent-working.jpg" alt="Prepare view with the cable holder on the bed and the chat showing Working on it"><figcaption><span class="n">1A-02</span> Six seconds after Open: Prepare, the model on the bed, the chat already working.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They opened one file. They expect to see it on a bed.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>Printer and filament chips in the header, a <span class="ui">Slice</span> button, the model on a Bambu textured plate, a right pane headed "New chat" with "Setup summary · 0.20mm Standard · Bambu PLA Basic · 9 settings changed · Not sliced yet · Agent working", a line "Opened universal_cable_holder_v2.3mf as a new project · you · 0…" and "Working on it… 3s".</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>"9 settings changed": by whom, from what? They just opened the file.</li><li>The plate says <span class="ui">Untitled</span> in orange while the window title is the file's name.</li><li>"Agent working" on what? There is no hint, only a timer.</li><li>The timestamp on the "Opened…" line is cut to "0…".</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1A-03-prepare-agent-first-message.jpg" alt="The agent's first message describing the cable holder with three answer buttons"><figcaption><span class="n">1A-03</span> Fifteen seconds later, the agent's first message.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They are waiting to see whether the app understood the file.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>"This is the Universal Cable Holder V2 with 4 clips. It's a small block, 38 × 19 × 15 mm… It was made for your A1 mini with a 0.4 mm nozzle, and it fits on the plate, so nothing needs attention. When I opened the file, I switched the filament setting from PLA Matte to PLA Basic to match the file… Should I slice it with these settings?" Buttons: Yes, slice it · Switch to PLA Matte · Not yet. The card now shows a <span class="ui">Compute estimates</span> link.</p></div>
    <div class="ok"><div class="k">Judgement</div><ul><li>This is the best moment in the product. Plain words, the right facts, one question, buttons.</li><li>"I switched the filament" is not true; the file carried that setting. Small, but it starts a wrong model of who acts.</li><li>The composer says "Or type your own answer…" with "Claude Opus 5.5" and "Thinking: Default" pickers; the person wonders whether they must choose.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1A-04-slicing-started-by-agent.jpg" alt="Slicing in progress with a progress card in the chat and the header showing Slicing"><figcaption><span class="n">1A-04</span> After "Yes, slice it".</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They said yes. Slicing takes a moment in any slicer.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>The chat renamed itself "Confirmation to Proceed With Slicing". "Worked for 3s · 1 action". "I've started slicing… It's using the designer's settings from the file. The main ones are 20% infill, slightly thinner outer lines and a different infill pattern." A card "Slicing plate 1 · plate 1:Generating walls · 15%" with Cancel. Header: "Slicing…" with an X. Card: "Time & material estimates recomputing…".</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>The chat title changed by itself to a sentence that reads like a form heading.</li><li>"plate 1:Generating walls" is OrcaSlicer's progress string, with its own punctuation.</li><li>The slice of a 38 mm part took over a minute on this debug build; the person would blame the app.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1A-05-slice-done-agent-report-and-issue-chip.jpg" alt="Slice finished: the agent's report with time and filament, a Print button, and a 1 print issue chip"><figcaption><span class="n">1A-05</span> Slice done. Print replaces Slice; a chip appears bottom right.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They expect a time estimate and to be told if anything is wrong.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>Card: "~22 min · 4.6 g · Plate 1 · $0.12". Chat: "The slice finished, and there's nothing to fix… I got one warning: the bed temperature is high enough that the filament might soften… inside a closed printer. Your A1 mini has no door or lid… you can ignore this warning. It's ready to print." Bottom right of the canvas: an orange dot and <span class="ui">1 print issue</span>.</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>"Nothing to fix" next to "1 print issue" with a warning colour. Which is it?</li><li>The report is good and the card figures are clear.</li><li>Nothing says where to look at the sliced result.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1A-06-print-button-menu.jpg" alt="Menu under the Print button: Check Print, Print all plates disabled, Export sliced file"><figcaption><span class="n">1A-06</span> The chevron beside Print.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>In Bambu Studio they would click Preview before printing.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>Check Print · Print all plates… (greyed, "1 sliced") · Export sliced file…</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>"Check Print" is findable but hidden under the action it should precede.</li><li>"Print all plates" is disabled with "1 sliced" and no reason; there is one plate.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1A-07-check-print-window.jpg" alt="Check print window with a layer preview, estimates, and a Checked nothing found list"><figcaption><span class="n">1A-07</span> Check print, a window over the canvas.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They want to see the layers and whether anything looks off.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>"Check print · Plate 1 · Tape_4.STL · sliced for Bambu Lab A1 mini 0.4 nozzle", a Legend checkbox, the sliced part with a layer slider (75 / 15.00 to 1 / 0.20), a right column: Time 22 min, Filament Bambu PLA Basic · 5 g · 1.5 m, Cost $0.12, "First layer · 1 object", "Checked, nothing found: supports (none generated), seams on marked faces, unsupported islands, crossing paths, paths outside the plate, OrcaSlicer warnings."</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>"Tape_4.STL" is a name they never saw; the file was called universal cable holder.</li><li>"Checked, nothing found … OrcaSlicer warnings" while the "1 print issue" chip is still lit beneath the window.</li><li>Otherwise a clear, calm screen. The layer slider is familiar.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1A-08-send-dialog-filament-mapping-error.jpg" alt="Send print job dialog with a red filament mapping error"><figcaption><span class="n">1A-08</span> Print opens OrcaSlicer's send dialog. Red text at once.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>The agent said "ready to print". They pressed Print.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>"Send print job": a render, "universal_cable_holder_v2 · 21m43s · 4.65 g", Printer "JusPrin Fake A1 mini(LAN) · Bambu Lab A1 mini 0.4mm", a Filament box with "PLA" and a red "!" dropdown, red text "Not all filaments used in slicing are mapped to the printer. Please check the mapping of filaments.", Timelapse On/Off, Auto Bed Leveling On/Off with "?", Send.</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>Ready, then an error. Nobody warned them about "mapping".</li><li>The dialog's wording, "?" help icons and toggles come from another product.</li><li>The chat is right there but cannot be used while this dialog is open.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1A-09-send-dialog-filament-mapping-popup.jpg" alt="Filament mapping popup listing AMS slot A1 with a question mark and an external spool"><figcaption><span class="n">1A-09</span> The "!" dropdown.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>PLA is in slot 1 of the AMS lite.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>"Nozzle · Please select from the following filaments · AMS: A1 ? (AMS Lite-1) · External: Ext ?".</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>Both choices are "?". The printer has not told the app what is loaded; the dialog does not say that.</li><li>The agent earlier said "make sure PLA is loaded", which is a different instruction from "map the slot".</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1A-11-send-unknown-filament-warning.jpg" alt="Warning dialog about unknown filaments in the AMS mappings with Confirm and Cancel"><figcaption><span class="n">1A-11</span> Slot picked, Send pressed: a "Warning" on top of the dialog.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They chose slot A1 and pressed Send.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>"There are some unknown filaments in the AMS mappings. Please check whether they are the required filaments. If they are okay, click Confirm to start printing." Confirm · Cancel.</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>Third dialog in a row for one Send. Each is a yes/no with consequences they cannot judge.</li><li>Partly caused by the fake printer's unknown spool, but a real AMS with an unread spool behaves the same.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1A-12-after-send-no-acknowledgement.jpg" alt="Prepare view after Send with no change in the chat or header"><figcaption><span class="n">1A-12</span> After Confirm. The log says the job was sent. The screen says nothing.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They pressed Confirm. Did it go?</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>Exactly the screen from before Print. No message, no state on the printer chip, the same "1 print issue".</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>Nothing confirms the send. With a real printer the first sign would be the machine moving.</li><li>Nothing in the chat records that a print went out, so later "what did I print?" has no answer here.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 id="s2a">Scenario 2A · the print issue chip after a slice</h2>
<p>Same project, right after the slice. This is the chip's natural moment: the agent said "ignore it", the chip says "1 print issue".</p>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/2A-01-print-issue-chip-popup.jpg" alt="Popup from the print issue chip with OrcaSlicer's heatbed warning and Ask AI, Resolve with AI, Ignore buttons"><figcaption><span class="n">2A-01</span> Clicking the chip.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>A badge with a number means something to deal with. The agent said the warning does not apply.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>"Warning · Whole plate · The current heatbed temperature is relatively high. The nozzle may clog when printing this filament in a closed environment. Please open the front door and/or remove the upper glass." Buttons <span class="ui">Ask AI</span>, <span class="ui">Resolve with AI</span>, link "Ignore this warning".</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>The advice is for a printer with a door and glass. Theirs has neither.</li><li>Ask vs Resolve: what would Resolve change? They cannot tell before clicking.</li><li>Three sources now disagree about this one warning: the chat, the chip, Check print.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/2A-02-ask-ai-posted-question.jpg" alt="The chat shows a message posted in the user's name quoting the warning"><figcaption><span class="n">2A-02</span> Ask AI posts a message as the person.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They clicked a button, not typed anything.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>A bubble on their side: "Explain this issue and my options. > The current heatbed temperature…" then "Working on it".</p></div>
    <div class="ok"><div class="k">Judgement</div><p>Acceptable. The words are put in their mouth, but they say what the button said, and the thread now has the context.</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/2A-03-ask-ai-answer-with-failed-action.jpg" alt="The agent explains the heatbed warning, says it does not apply, and offers three options; the activity row says 1 failed"><figcaption><span class="n">2A-03</span> The answer.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They want to know if it is safe to print.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>"What the warning means… Why it doesn't apply to you… Your options: Print as is (what I recommend) · If the printer sits inside an enclosure… · Lower the bed temperature… I haven't changed anything." Buttons for each. Above it: "Worked for 4s · 1 action · 1 failed".</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>"1 failed", unexplained, under an answer they are about to trust.</li><li>"I show this warning for every PLA print": the agent did not show it, OrcaSlicer did.</li><li>The chip still says "1 print issue" after "Print as is"; it never clears.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 id="s1b">Scenario 1B · a bare STL with no settings in it</h2>
<p>File: "general purpose hook", a Printables-style STL with no profile. The person has to say what it is for, and the agent has to plan from nothing.</p>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1B-02-prepare-phantom-edits-attributed-to-you.jpg" alt="New project chat opening with nine settings-change rows attributed to you, then the Opened line and Working on it"><figcaption><span class="n">1B-02</span> The new project, six seconds in.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They opened a plain STL. They changed no settings today.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>Nine rows before anything else: "Sparse infill density 20% → 15% · you, in 0.20mm Standard @BBL A1M · 05:56", "Sparse infill pattern Cross Zag → Cross Hatch", "Type Normal (auto) → Tree (auto)", "First layer 0.36 mm → 0.5 mm", "Inherits profile 0.20mm Standard @BBL A1M → Empty", "print_settings_id HSW_Kabelhalter → Empty". Then "Opened general_purpose_hook.stl as a new project · you".</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>"you" on nine changes they did not make.</li><li>Raw keys (<span class="ui">print_settings_id</span>), "Empty", "@BBL A1M", and "HSW_Kabelhalter", a name from the designer of the previous file.</li><li>They cannot tell whether this damaged anything or whether to undo it. The rows are what the previous project's embedded profile left behind when the app went back to the stock preset.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1B-03-agent-asks-purpose.jpg" alt="The agent says the hook loaded cleanly, warns about layer direction, and asks what it will hold with three buttons"><figcaption><span class="n">1B-03</span> The first message.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They want to print the hook; they have not said what for.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>"Your wall hook loaded cleanly: one solid piece, 20 × 45 × 28 mm… Standing like this, the hook's arm is built up in stacked layers, and that's where a loaded hook usually snaps. For anything heavy, I might lay it on its side… What will this hook be holding?" Buttons: Light stuff like keys · A coat or bag · Something heavy. A small toolbar (move, rotate, scale, copy, …) has appeared at the top left of the canvas.</p></div>
    <div class="ok"><div class="k">Judgement</div><ul><li>Exactly the right question, in their words, with the reason.</li><li>The floating toolbar appeared because the object got selected; no label says what it is.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1B-04-agent-working-setup-card-and-reorient.jpg" alt="The setup card shows the purpose and plan headline, the hook now lies on its side, the chat shows Working on it 34s"><figcaption><span class="n">1B-04</span> After typing the purpose: 34 seconds of work, visible on the canvas.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They typed "garage wall, bike helmet, 1.5 kg, sturdy, looks don't matter".</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>The card now reads "Garage wall hook for a 1.5 kg bike helmet · Printed on its side for strength, thick walls, supports accepted · 5 settings changed". The hook has been laid on its side on the bed. Rows "Place object" and "5 settings" are collapsed. "Working on it… 34s".</p></div>
    <div class="ok"><div class="k">Judgement</div><ul><li>Their words come back on the card and the model moved: the two best signals the app has.</li><li>34 seconds with only a timer is long; the collapsed rows say what, not why.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1B-06-plan-message-scrolled.jpg" alt="The plan message: supports on, walls 2 to 5, infill 15 to 40 percent, brim, two caveats about heat and the screw"><figcaption><span class="n">1B-06</span> The plan, scrolled up.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They want to know what was done and why.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>"Supports turned on… Thicker and denser: outer walls from 2 to 5, inside fill from 15% to 40%… Brim added… I kept your PLA… Two things to keep in mind: Heat (PLA softens in a hot garage; PETG holds up better) · The screw."</p></div>
    <div class="ok"><div class="k">Judgement</div><p>Readable by someone who knows walls and infill, and still readable by someone who does not. The caveats are the kind a friend would give.</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/1B-05-auto-sliced-report-3-issues-chip.jpg" alt="The agent sliced on its own and reports 28 minutes, 6.6 g, two warnings; the chip says 3 print issues"><figcaption><span class="n">1B-05</span> It sliced without asking, then reported.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>Last time it asked before slicing.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>"The slice finished and your helmet hook is ready to print… I also got two warnings, and neither needs action… After printing, snap off the support props and peel off the brim." Card: "~28 min · 6.6 g · Plate 1 · $0.17". Chip: <span class="ui">3 print issues</span>. The chat is now titled "Sturdy Garage Wall Bike Helmet Hook".</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>Two warnings in the chat, three in the chip.</li><li>It did not ask this time. Fine on its own; the inconsistency with 1A is the problem.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 id="s2c">Scenario 2C · the person changes the filament by hand</h2>
<p>Same hook project, plan recorded. The agent mentioned heat and PETG; the person reaches for the filament chip themselves.</p>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/2C-01-filament-chip-menu.jpg" alt="Filament chip menu with colour swatches, installed filaments, Other filament, Filament settings, Calibrate, Add a slot"><figcaption><span class="n">2C-01</span> The filament chip menu.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>The agent said PETG holds heat better. They want something heat resistant.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>"Filament on Bambu Lab A1 mini · 0.4", a Colour row of four swatches and "+", Installed: Bambu PLA Basic ✓, Bambu PLA Matte, Generic ABS, ASA, PA, PA-CF, PC; then Other filament…, Filament settings…, Calibrate this filament…, Add a slot.</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>No PETG. "Other filament…" might have it, but ABS is right there, so they pick ABS.</li><li>What do the colour swatches do? Nothing explains them.</li><li>"Add a slot" means the AMS slot count, which is not obvious.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/2C-02-after-manual-abs-plan-needs-another-look.jpg" alt="After choosing Generic ABS the card shows Plan needs another look and Previous estimates not current; the menu is still open; the chat is unchanged"><figcaption><span class="n">2C-02</span> Generic ABS chosen. Ten seconds later.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They changed one thing. They expect the app to react, or at least the agent to comment, since it had an opinion on heat.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>The chip says Generic ABS. The card gains "⚠ Plan needs another look · Generic ABS @System" and "⚠ Previous estimates · not current · Recompute". Print turns back into Slice. The menu is still open. The chat has not moved.</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><ul><li>"Needs another look" by whom? There is no button to ask for it.</li><li>"@System" is a preset-library term.</li><li>The agent, which argued for PLA a minute ago, says nothing.</li><li>The menu stays open after the choice.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/2C-03-manual-slice-with-abs-agent-silent.jpg" alt="After a manual Slice with ABS: Print button back, 27 min, 2 print issues, the card still says plan needs another look, the chat silent"><figcaption><span class="n">2C-03</span> They press Slice themselves. Done, with ABS, and still silence.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They are about to print ABS on an open printer with a plan written for PLA.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>Print is back, "~27 min · 5.2 g · $0.10", <span class="ui">2 print issues</span>, the card still flagged, no new message.</p></div>
    <div class="bad"><div class="k">What confuses them</div><p>Nothing stops them. The agent has an opinion (see the next frame) and the app knows the setup moved, but the person has to think to ask.</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <figure><img src="shots/2C-05-agent-answer-on-abs.jpg" alt="The agent explains ABS warps, cracks and smells on an open A1 mini and offers Switch back to PLA, Keep ABS, or I have a PETG spool"><figcaption><span class="n">2C-05</span> After "Is it still fine to print like this?": the answer, once scrolled to.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="lens">
    <div><div class="k">What they know</div><p>They asked a plain question.</p></div>
    <div><div class="k">What they see</div><p>"The filament setting is now Generic ABS, and the plate has already been re-sliced with it… The catch is your printer. The A1 mini is open… Warping · Weak layers · Fumes… My suggestion: switch back to PLA… PETG is the better heat-resistant option… Also check which spool is actually loaded." Buttons: Switch back to PLA · Keep ABS, I'll risk it · I have a PETG spool.</p></div>
    <div class="ok"><div class="k">Judgement</div><ul><li>Right content, right tone, honest about what the printer does not report.</li><li>The chat did not scroll to it; the view stayed on the old plan and the answer was far below (frame 2C-04).</li><li>The card's "needs another look" vanished when the agent started working, before the person chose.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</div>

